.TH task-iterative 5 2026-10-20 "${PACKAGE_STRING}" "User Manuals"

.SH NAME
task-iterative \- Iterative tasks for the Taskwarrior command line todo manager.

.SH DESCRIPTION
An iterative task repeats on a schedule. A series has one live task at a
time. Completing it closes that task and creates the next one in the series,
with its dates moved forward to the next interval.

Iterative tasks are an alternative to recurring tasks. A task cannot be both
recurring and iterative.

.SH CREATING AN ITERATIVE TASK
A task becomes iterative when it has all of:

.TP
.B iter:<interval>
How often the task repeats. See INTERVALS below.

.TP
.B iter_type:fixed|fixed+|chained
How the next date is chosen. See ITERATION TYPES below.

.TP
At least one of due, scheduled or wait
The date the schedule is anchored on.

.RE
For example:

.nf
    $ task add Pay rent iter:monthly iter_type:fixed due:2026-11-20
    $ task add Take out the bins iter:weekly iter_type:fixed+ due:friday
    $ task add Haircut iter:6wk iter_type:chained scheduled:today
.fi

Adding iter and iter_type to an existing pending or waiting task makes it
iterative.

.SH ITERATION TYPES

.TP
.B fixed
The next due date is the next interval from when the task was originally due.
If rent is due on the 20th of the month, the next due date is the 20th of the
next month, whether it was paid on the 15th, the 20th or the 25th.

.TP
.B fixed+
The next due date is the next interval from when the task was originally due,
on or after now. Missed intervals are skipped. If the garbage bins go out every
week but two weeks were missed while on vacation, they do not need to go out
several days in a row to catch up.

.TP
.B chained
The next due date is the next interval from when the task was completed. If a
haircut is due every six weeks and one was done today, the next is six weeks
from today, even if this one was three weeks late or two days early.

.RE
Short forms are also accepted: fx for fixed, f+ or fp for fixed+, and ch for
chained.

.SH INTERVALS
The iter value can be written in any of these forms. They are tried in this
order:

.TP
Raw RRULE
An RFC 5545 recurrence rule, such as "FREQ=WEEKLY;BYDAY=MO,TH".

.TP
Taskwarrior durations
The same values accepted by recur, such as daily, weekdays, 3wk, fortnight
or 2year. See
.BR task (1).

.TP
ISO-8601 durations
Such as P2W, P3D or PT12H. Months or years cannot be mixed with smaller units,
and weeks cannot be combined with any other unit.

.TP
Natural language
Such as "every monday", "every other tuesday", "the last friday of the month"
or "every two weeks on friday". Multi-word values must be quoted.

.SH DATES
The anchor is the first of due, scheduled and wait that is set. When a task is
completed, the next date is computed for the anchor, and due, scheduled, wait
and until are all moved forward by the same amount, which keeps the spacing
between them.

The until date applies to a single task, not the series. To end a series on a
date, use an RRULE with UNTIL.

.SH SERIES POSITION
Each task in a series has a read-only iter_count attribute holding its position
in the series. The first task is 1 and each new task adds one. An RRULE with
COUNT ends the series once iter_count reaches COUNT.

Duplicating an iterative task starts a new series at position 1.

.SH ENDING A SERIES
A series ends on its own when an RRULE runs out through COUNT or UNTIL. The last
task completes normally and no new task is created.

To end a series early, either:

.TP
Delete the current task
Deleting does not create a new task, so the series stops there.

.TP
Clear iter
For example "task 1 modify iter:". The task becomes an ordinary pending task
that can be completed or deleted like any other. Its iter_type and iter_count
are removed, so adding iter again starts a new series at 1.

.RE
While a task is iterative, iter_type cannot be removed, and the last of due,
scheduled and wait cannot be removed.

.SH REPORTS AND FILTERS
An iterative task appears in reports like a pending task. A future wait date
hides it like any other waiting task.

.TP
.B status:iterative
Matches iterative tasks.

.TP
.B +ITERATIVE
Matches iterative tasks.

.TP
.B +PENDING
Matches pending and iterative tasks that are not waiting.

.SH SYNCHRONIZATION
If the same task is completed on two replicas before they sync, both create the
same next task, so the series does not end up with duplicates.

The next date is computed in the local timezone of the replica that completes
the task.

